llins edge and a slight Jackson lead, and the statewide average shows Jackson ahead by only a point or two. Collins carries baggage from her Supreme Court votes in a state that has trended Democratic, while Jackson faces questions about his own record and a late candidate-contrast controversy.\n\nCollins holds a large fundraising advantage and her incumbency has historically translated into outperformance, but the national and state environment favors Democrats. Expert raters mostly call the race a Tossup, while several model-based outlets and prediction markets tilt toward Jackson. The race remains genuinely competitive with control of the Senate potentially at stake.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.7341, "margin": 3.16}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.6906, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.626, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.6915, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.5751, "margin": 1.5}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.6364, "margin": 2.5}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.6474, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.6668, "margin": 2.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.6495, "margin": 2.0}]}